<!-- Keep the keyboard focus ring keyboard-only.

     On Windows/Linux, a mouse click on a <button> focuses it. (macOS browsers
     deliberately do not, which is why this was invisible on Chrome/FF/Safari
     there and showed up only in Edge.) The button then *keeps* focus — and this
     app binds global keyboard shortcuts, so the user's next keypress makes
     Chromium re-evaluate :focus-visible against the still-focused button and
     paint a ring around a control they clicked ten seconds ago. That is the
     "Sort" and typeface-chevron rectangle.

     Suppressing the default action of `pointerdown` stops a button taking focus
     from a pointer. Per the Pointer Events spec this does NOT suppress `click`,
     so every handler still runs; it only prevents focus and the compatibility
     `mousedown`. Keyboard activation (Enter/Space) fires no pointer event at
     all, so Tab users are untouched and still get the ring from
     kanji-alive-modern.css.

     Scoped to <button>: the custom scrollbar thumb (search.ejs) is a <div> that
     drags via @mousedown, and must keep its default behaviour.

     Delegated on `document` so it survives the fetch()+innerHTML and
     fetch()+replaceWith() swaps that rebuild the page's controls. -->
<script>
  document.addEventListener('pointerdown', function (e) {
    if (e.target instanceof Element && e.target.closest('button')) {
      e.preventDefault();
    }
  });
</script>
